# .gitattributes — byte-level git policy for suno-master-utility-browser-extension
# =============================================================================
#
# Two rules. The first is hygiene. The second is load-bearing, and it is the
# reason this file exists at all.
#
# WHY .gitignore COULD NOT DO THIS JOB
# ------------------------------------
# vendor/README.md records a SHA-256 for each vendored encoder and states that
# those hashes are "only meaningful while the bytes are exactly what upstream
# published":
#
#     vendor/lame.all.js          530,087 B  026bd888...a3b
#     vendor/OggVorbisEncoder.js  2,358,493 B  5a9f749a...79b
#
# vendor/ is also what discharges the LGPL-3.0 obligation described there: the
# library must stay a separate, *unmodified* work that a user can replace
# without relinking. Both arguments rest on the file being byte-identical to
# upstream on every machine that checks the repo out.
#
# That is not something .gitignore can protect. .gitignore controls MEMBERSHIP
# — which paths git tracks — and has no say whatsoever over the BYTES of the
# files it does track. A tracked file is rewritten in the working tree on
# checkout according to the EOL rules; whether or not it is listed in
# .gitignore is irrelevant. So the only place to state "these files are
# binary, leave them alone" is .gitattributes.
#
# THE FAILURE THIS PREVENTS
# -------------------------
# Under `core.autocrlf=input` (the macOS default) git applies no EOL
# normalisation at all, so today's checkout is already correct. The danger is
# the next contributor: anyone on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true` (still the
# legacy default on a fresh Git for Windows install), or anyone whose editor
# rewrites line endings on save, checks out 2.8 MB of CRLF-lifted JavaScript.
# Every LF becomes CRLF, the stored blob changes, the recorded SHA-256 stops
# matching, and check-build.sh fails on a file nobody touched — or worse, the
# integrity claim is quietly believed rather than verified. Rule 1 below stops
# that for every file in the repo except the two that must not be touched at
# all; rule 2 covers those.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RULE 1
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto eol=lf
#
#   text=auto  Let git detect text vs binary per file (NUL in the first 8000
#              bytes means binary), and normalise line endings on check-in so
#              the STORED blob is always LF regardless of who committed it or
#              what their platform did.
#   eol=lf     Check the file out with LF on every platform, and fail loudly
#              rather than silently converting if a CRLF ever reaches the
#              working tree.
#
# This is the LF invariant the extension pages expect. The offscreen document
# in particular runs an asm.js bundle whose memory initialiser is a blob of
# hex bytes — but `eol=lf` is still the right default for the 22 extension
# text files, which are hand-written and diff-friendly.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RULE 2
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
vendor/** -text
#
#   -text  Undo every part of rule 1 for this subtree. Mark the files BINARY:
#          no EOL conversion on check-in, no EOL conversion on check-out, no
#          CRLF filtering, no "safecrlf" rewriting. The working tree bytes are
#          the blob bytes are the upstream bytes on macOS, Linux and Windows
#          alike.
#
# This is what protects the SHA-256 record in vendor/README.md and, with it,
# the LGPL "unmodified separate work" claim that offscreen/offscreen.js relies
# on when it loads the encoder as a plain <script src>.
#
# WHY `vendor/** text eol=lf` IS NOT ENOUGH FOR THIS DIRECTORY
# -----------------------------------------------------------
# `text` is not a synonym for "leave line endings alone". When git's text
# heuristic classifies a file as text, its normaliser maps CR, LF and CRLF all
# to a single LF. In other words `eol=lf` would ALSO rewrite a LONE CR — a
# carriage return with no following newline, exactly the byte sequence an old
# Mac (CR-only, pre-OS X) or a mangled concatenation produces — into a plain LF,
# silently, with no CRLF anywhere in sight for a human to notice.
#
# That matters because vendor/OggVorbisEncoder.js is an **asm.js** bundle
# (see vendor/README.md: one `use asm` directive, zero WebAssembly
# references) carrying an EMBEDDED MEMORY INITIALISER — a large run of
# machine-code-shaped byte values baked into the JavaScript. This is precisely
# the class of payload a stray CR corrupts silently: the file still parses,
# the file is still "text", the SHA-256 recorded next to it simply stops
# matching, and the failure surfaces much later as an Ogg export producing
# garbage or throwing inside libvorbis rather than as an encoding bug.
#
# A hash check in scripts/check-build.sh would catch the damage, but the point
# of this file is to stop the damage happening: `-text` makes the corruption
# impossible rather than merely detectable.
#
# VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED
# ---------------------
# Checked the actual bytes before writing this claim down. As of this commit
# `vendor/OggVorbisEncoder.js` contains ZERO CR bytes of any kind:
#
#     $ grep -c $'\r' vendor/OggVorbisEncoder.js
#     0
#
# and a byte census gives 2,358,493 bytes, 37,310 LF, 0 CR, 0 CRLF. The same
# is true of vendor/lame.all.js (530,087 B, 15,518 LF, 0 CR, 0 CRLF).
#
# So the lone-CR hazard described above is PROSPECTIVE, not present: these
# files are upstream-clean right now, and this rule keeps them that way. It is
# a statement about what must never be allowed to happen to them, not a report
# of something already broken.